#
# prima-materia — the Hippocratic transmission, registered and entered at once
#
# This file exists because prima-materia#612 asked for it by name. That issue
# holds a convergence candidate — two transmissions coupling a wounded site to
# a counted interval of death — and it names the counter-comparandum the
# candidate needs: Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.18, a list of parts whose severe
# wounding is deadly, with no interval anywhere in it. Without that list the
# convergence would be a statement about two corpora that happen to count
# days. With it, the thing being claimed can be stated: that the interval and
# not the organ list is the distinguishing feature.
#
# Registration and working are two acts (SPEC §14), and here they fall
# together because the passage was read before the file was written. The three
# decisions §14 requires before a first node are therefore made here and not
# left implicit.
#
# One: the contact route is documented and is not close. Galen commented on
# these aphorisms sentence by sentence, the Arabic and Latin medicine descend
# from the commentary, and the corpora this holding already carries in the
# Mediterranean sphere — the Graeco-Roman witnesses, the Platonic and
# Neoplatonic material, the hesychast literature — stand downstream of or
# beside the same stream. A witness from here raises the number of files and
# not the number of independent witnesses within that sphere. Against the
# Indian and Chinese medical corpora the route is a separate and open question
# and is held at the convergence node, not here.
#
# Two: the edition. What was read is the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine
# Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, printed for the Sydenham Society, London
# 1849, in the copy archive.org carries as b33291408_0004. It is a translation
# and not a critical text: Littré and the Loeb were not consulted, and the
# Greek is not present on this route at all. Every node below says so in its
# source.
#
# Three: how many citable passages there are. Aphorisms, Section VI was read
# continuously, all sixty aphorisms. Seven of them are entered. What the other
# fifty-three are and why they are not entered stands in the harvest note at
# prima-materia#829.
#
# On the identifiers, because the rule in AGENTS.md does not quite reach this
# case. Identifiers are to be formed from the technical term in the original
# language and expressly not from the English or German translation. The
# original language of this corpus is Greek and the route that was available
# does not carry it; Adams prints the Greek for a handful of terms and for
# none of these aphorisms. The identifiers below are therefore Latin, which is
# the form in which this corpus has been cited for a thousand years and the
# form a second run reading the same volume would reach for. It is a worse
# anchor than a read Greek term and a better one than an English phrase, and
# it is recorded here rather than passed over.
#
# No page numbers. The body text of this scan is clean, and the digits in its
# running heads are not: the page numbers over Section VI read 753, 759, 757,
# 799 and 761 in that order, which is OCR noise and not a page sequence. The
# aphorisms are cited by section and number, which is the canonical handle of
# this work and locates the passage in any edition. The access-path finding is
# entered in the table in AGENTS.md.
#

pmt:HippocraticMedicine a pm:Tradition ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Hippocratic medicine"@en, "Hippokratische Medizin"@de ;
    skos:definition "The Greek medical transmission of the collection that travels under the name of Hippocrates, taken here at the part of it that states outcomes: which conditions and injuries are said to be fatal, which signs are said to decide a case, and at what term, if any, the outcome is said to arrive."@en ;
    skos:note "Read at the wording in the run of 2026-10-02: Aphorisms, Section VI, all sixty aphorisms, in the English of Francis Adams, vol. II, London 1849. Not read then: Sections I to V and VII of the same Aphorisms, which stand in the same volume and were opened only far enough to establish where Section VI begins and ends; and the rest of the collection, among it the Prognostics, On Wounds in the Head, On the Articulations and the Epidemics, which the same two volumes carry. The works below say which of them were opened."@en ;
    skos:note "Read at the wording in the run of 2026-10-03: The Book of Prognostics, chapters 1 to 25, and On Injuries of the Head, chapters 1 to 21, both continuously, in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849, pp. 234-256 and 445-466, over the archive.org full text genuineworksofhi01hippuoft; the Greek of the Prognostic alongside from the Loeb volume hippocrates02hippuoft. Two of the three named works are now read and the third, the Aphorisms, is read in one section of eight. The coverage state stays at places entered: a collection of some sixty treatises of which three chapters-worth has been opened is not a harvested corpus, and the two treatises added here are the two that were already named as identifiers rather than two chosen freely."@en ;
    skos:note "A correction to the run of 2026-10-02, and it is the kind that costs a later reader an hour. The note above says the Prognostics and On Wounds in the Head stand in the same two volumes, which is true, but the work notes below had left a reader to guess which; both are in volume I and only the Aphorisms are in volume II. Volume II of this translation is also the copy whose running-head digits over Section VI are noise, while volume I in the copy read on 2026-10-03 has a monotone head sequence over both treatises, so page numbers are used below and were not used above."@en ;
    skos:note "What kind of witness this is. The Aphorisms are a list of sentences with no argument between them, assembled from the other treatises of the collection and transmitted as a set to be learnt; Adams's own preliminary discourse in volume I treats the eighth section as not belonging to the rest. A sentence here is therefore a claim that the transmission thought worth fixing in a form that could be memorised, which is a different thing from a claim it thought worth demonstrating, and nothing in this file should be read as the latter."@en ;
    skos:note "The commentary is not the text. Adams prints Galen's remarks under most aphorisms and sometimes his own judgment on whether the sentence still holds; at VI.18 Galen is reported as saying that wounds of the brain, liver, bowels and bladder are not necessarily fatal and as citing lithotomy against the bladder. That is a later station qualifying the claim, it is reported here and not entered as a node, and the reason is that Galen was read through Adams and not opened. A run that wants the qualification as a node opens the commentary."@en ;
    pm:principalCorpus "The Corpus Hippocraticum: the Aphorisms; the Prognostics; On Wounds in the Head; On the Articulations; the Epidemics; and the remaining treatises of the collection" ;
    pm:corpusWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis, pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis, pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    pm:coverageState pm:placesEntered ;
    pm:contactRoute pm:contactRouteDocumented ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.18, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

#
# The works of this corpus as identifiers
#

pmw:AphorismiHippocratis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hippocrates, Aphorisms"@en, "Hippokrates, Aphorismen"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-02 at Section VI, read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. II, London 1849. Sections I to V and VII were not read and stand in the same volume, so the cheapest continuation of this file is the same volume: Section VII carries the acute prognostic signs and Section V the material on women and on the seasons, and both bear on questions this holding already asks of other corpora."@en ;
    skos:note "The Greek is not on this route. Anyone who needs the wording of a term here goes to Littré or to the Loeb, and until someone does, every node formed from this work is a node about a nineteenth-century English rendering of a Greek sentence."@en .

pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hippocrates, Prognostics"@en, "Hippokrates, Prognostikon"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-03, chapters 1 to 25, read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849, pp. 234-256, with the Greek alongside from the Loeb volume of 1923. Not read: the Argument Adams sets before it, pp. 223-233, and the Appendix he prints after it, pp. 257-270, which he himself holds to be spurious. The question this work was named for is answered and the answer is in the other work of this run: the term for a wound is in On Injuries of the Head and not here, and what rides on a number here is the course of a disease."@en ;
    skos:note "The cheapest continuation from this work is its own appendix and the treatise that stands before it in the same volume. On Regimen in Acute Diseases, pp. 271-336, is where this corpus describes evacuating, bathing and the conditions of the patient under which a measure is or is not to be applied, and the holding has an open question that goes exactly there: the catalogue of states in which an operation on a vessel is forbidden, searched for in these two treatises and not found, at pmp:HippocraticVulnusStatusSearch."@en .

pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis a dcterms:BibliographicResource ;
    rdfs:label "Hippocrates, On Wounds in the Head"@en, "Hippokrates, Über die Kopfwunden"@de ;
    skos:note "Opened on 2026-10-03, chapters 1 to 21, read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849, pp. 445-466. It was named as the strongest candidate in this corpus for a stated interval after an injury and it carries one; the finding changed shape in the reading rather than collapsing, and what it is now stands at pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni. Not read: Adams's Argument before it, pp. 423-444, and the three plates of instruments at the end of the volume."@en ;
    skos:note "The Greek is not on this route for this work, and the failure is the dangerous kind. The Loeb volume hippocrates03hippuoft prints the treatise with its Greek, and its archive.org full text carries zero characters in U+0370-U+03FF and U+1F00-U+1FFF: the OCR has rendered every Greek word in lookalike Latin letters, so the file looks clean and the original language is absent. Littre's third volume, BIUSante_34859x03, does carry Greek characters, about 240000 of them, but systematically misread. Anyone needing the wording of a term here goes to the critical edition."@en .

#
# The list of fatal parts, which is the counter-comparandum
#

pmc:HippocratesVulneraLetalia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The parts whose severe wounding is deadly, named without a term"@en, "Die Teile, deren schwere Verwundung tödlich ist, ohne Frist genannt"@de ;
    skos:definition "Seven parts are named and the claim made of each is the same: a severe wound of the bladder, of the brain, of the heart, of the diaphragm, of the small intestines, of the stomach, and of the liver is deadly. The sentence gives a site and an outcome. It gives no interval, no sign by which the case is to be recognised while the outcome is pending, and no gradation among the seven."@en ;
    skos:note "This node is in the holding for what it does not say. Two other transmissions couple a wounded site to a counted interval of death — the needling-prohibition chapter of the Suwen, with six organs and six day-counts, and the marman doctrine of the Susruta, with five classes and their terms — and the agreement between those two is worth nothing until it is known whether coupling a site to an interval is simply what a medical transmission does with a wound. Here is one that does not."@en ;
    skos:note "And the absence is not a limit of the genre, which is why it is worth entering rather than merely noting. The same Section VI counts days twice: a man suddenly seized with pains in the head and laid down speechless dies in seven days unless fever come on, and gouty inflammation subsides in the course of forty days. This corpus can state a term. It states none for a wound."@en ;
    skos:note "What the aphorism would need in order to fail, and it has it: a severe wound of one of these seven parts that is not deadly. Galen, as Adams reports him, says exactly that of four of the seven, and the holding keeps his qualification out of the graph because he was read through the translator and not opened."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.18, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

pmc:HippocratesIntestinumTransfixum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "A transfixed small intestine does not unite"@en, "Ein durchbohrter Dünndarm wächst nicht zusammen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Stated of the part and not of the patient: if any of the small intestines be transfixed, it does not unite. The neighbouring aphorism says the same of a bone, a cartilage, a nerve and the slender part of the jaw, which once cut out do not grow again."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered beside the list of fatal parts because it is the same operation at one remove. It names a site, it names an outcome, and the outcome is an absence — the wound does not close — which is a thing an observer could in principle be wrong about and no term is set for the observation."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.24, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

#
# The two places in the section that do count days
#

pmc:HippocratesSeptemDies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Death in seven days, unless fever come on"@en, "Tod binnen sieben Tagen, wenn nicht Fieber hinzutritt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one counted term in the section attached to a sudden event in a previously healthy person: persons in good health suddenly seized with pains in the head, and straightway laid down speechless and breathing with stertor, die in seven days — unless fever come on."@en ;
    skos:note "The structure is the whole value of the node. A term is named, so a moment arrives at which the claim has either held or not; and a release is named in the same sentence, so the case in which the patient is alive on the eighth day is covered in advance if a fever appeared. The transmission put both halves in one line."@en ;
    skos:note "It is also the reason the silence at VI.18 counts. This corpus does state intervals, and when it does it states them with an exception; what it never does in this section is state one for a wound."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.51, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

pmc:HippocratesQuadragintaDies a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The gouty inflammation subsides in forty days"@en, "Die gichtische Entzündung geht in vierzig Tagen zurück"@de ;
    skos:definition "Stated without condition and without a remedy attached: in gouty affections, the inflammation subsides in the course of forty days."@en ;
    skos:note "The second counted term in the section, and the cleaner of the two, because nothing is said to intervene and nothing is said to excuse a longer course. A claim of this form is settled by counting, and the aphorism offers no account of a case that runs past the fortieth day."@en ;
    skos:note "The same section says, two aphorisms later, that gouty affections for the most part rankle in spring and in autumn, which is a second claim about the same disease on a different clock. The two are not reconciled in the text and are not reconciled here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.49, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

pmc:HippocratesCerebrumVulneratum a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The sign that must follow a severe wound of the brain"@en, "Das Zeichen, das einer schweren Hirnwunde folgen muss"@de ;
    skos:definition "When the brain is severely wounded, fever and vomiting of bile necessarily supervene. The claim is not about the outcome but about a sign, and it is made with a necessity: these two things follow, and not for the most part."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the nearest the section comes to what the Suwen does at every one of its six organs, where each interval is paired with a bodily movement by which the strike is to be recognised. Here the sign stands alone, with no interval beside it, and it is stated of one of the seven parts the list of fatal wounds names."@en ;
    skos:note "A necessity is cheap to refute and expensive to state, which is why the form is worth recording: a severely wounded brain without fever and without bilious vomiting is outside the sentence."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.50, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

#
# Venesection, which the section touches twice
#

pmp:HippocratesVenaeSectio a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Opening a vein"@en, "Das Öffnen einer Vene"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation as the section states it, which is in two sentences and no more: venesection cures dysuria, and the internal veins of the arm are the ones to open; and persons who are benefited by venesection or purging should be bled or purged in spring. No posture, no quantity, no instrument, no sign of a good opening and no list of persons who are not to be opened."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered with the thinness showing, because the thinness is the comparison. The Susruta devotes a whole lesson of its anatomical book to this same operation — the persons unfit for it, the three times of day and year at which it may be done, the appearance of a well-pierced vein, the quantity to be taken, twenty named ways of opening a vein badly — and the Aphorisms give two lines. Whether the rest of the Hippocratic collection carries the apparatus is a question for the treatises this file names and does not open."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.36 and VI.47, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

pmc:HippocratesVenaeSectioVere a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Bleed and purge in spring"@en, "Im Frühjahr zur Ader und abführen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Persons who are benefited by venesection or purging should be bled or purged in spring. The season is stated as the time of the operation and no consequence is given for operating outside it."@en ;
    skos:note "A season without a stated penalty. The holding carries a number of claims that bind an operation to a calendar and the useful distinction among them is whether anything is said to follow from acting outside the window; here nothing is, and the node is entered at that strength rather than at a stronger one."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesVenaeSectio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.47, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

pmp:HippocratesIncisioEtCauterium a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Treating empyema or dropsy by incision or the cautery"@en, "Die Behandlung von Empyem oder Wassersucht durch Schnitt oder Brenneisen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation the section names only in order to state the condition under which it kills: cases of empyema or dropsy treated by incision or by the cautery, so that the collected water or pus is let out. Nothing is said about where the incision is made, with what, or how much is to be taken."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the caution beside it needs something to warn about and because the act is named in the text rather than supplied here. The thinness is the same thinness as at the venesection node and has the same explanation: the Aphorisms assemble sentences and leave the procedures to the treatises they were assembled from."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.27, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

pmc:HippocratesEvacuatioCeleris a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:AphorismiHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The evacuation that kills by being quick"@en, "Die Entleerung, die durch ihre Schnelligkeit tötet"@de ;
    skos:definition "Those cases of empyema or dropsy which are treated by incision or the cautery, if the water or the pus flow rapidly all at once, certainly prove fatal. The claim is about the manner of the operation and not about the disease: the same incision kills or does not kill according to the rate at which it empties."@en ;
    skos:note "The sharpest caution in the section and the only one that puts the outcome on the operator's hand. It is the Greek counterpart of what the Susruta states about quantity in its lesson on venesection, where an excessive flow is forbidden and a remnant of the diseased blood is to be left standing on purpose."@en ;
    skos:note "A second caution of the same family stands nine aphorisms earlier and is not entered separately: when a person has been cured of chronic hemorrhoids, unless one be left, there is danger of dropsy or phthisis supervening. Both sentences say that the complete removal is the dangerous one."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesIncisioEtCauterium ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, Aphorisms VI.27, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. II, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849" .

#
# — The Book of Prognostics, chapters 1 to 25, opened 2026-10-03 —
#
# Read continuously in the English of Francis Adams, vol. I, London 1849,
# over the archive.org full text genuineworksofhi01hippuoft. Both treatises
# of this run stand in that volume and not in the second, which is where the
# Aphorisms are; the earlier note at pmw:AphorismiHippocratis sent a reader
# to the wrong volume for them and is corrected above.
#
# The Greek is on this route for this treatise and not for the other. The
# Loeb volume hippocrates02hippuoft carries the Prognostic with its
# polytonic accents intact in the full text, measured at 120900 characters in
# U+0370-U+03FF and 23617 in U+1F00-U+1FFF; the Loeb volume for the head
# wounds, hippocrates03hippuoft, carries zero characters in either block and
# renders the Greek in lookalike Latin letters, which is the failure class
# AGENTS.md records at prima-materia#775. So the terms below are given in
# Greek where the Prognostic supplies them and not at all for the other work.
#
# The identifiers stay Latin. The file established that convention on
# 2026-10-02 because the Greek was not on the route, and changing it now
# would split one corpus across two naming schemes for the sake of one
# treatise; SPEC §11 forbids renaming coined identifiers without a
# compelling reason and this is not one. The Greek term is carried as
# skos:altLabel at each node where it was read, so that a run reaching for
# the Greek first still finds the node.
#
# Page numbers are used. The running-head sequence over both treatises was
# checked in this scan and is monotone from 226 to 256 and from 424 to 466,
# with one single-digit OCR slip in it — the head over the page between 246
# and 248 reads 217 — which is reconstructible and not noise. That is the
# opposite of what the same translation's second volume gave at
# b33291408_0004, where the heads over Section VI read 753, 759, 757, 799,
# 761. The access-path finding stands in the table in AGENTS.md and this
# measurement confirms its last sentence: the failure hangs on the copy, not
# on the route.
#

pmc:HippocratesPronoia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Foreknowledge, as this corpus defines the physician's business"@en, "Die Vorauskenntnis, wie dieser Korpus das Geschäft des Arztes bestimmt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "πρόνοια"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The treatise opens by naming a capacity and its scope: it is an excellent thing for the physician to cultivate foreknowledge, for by foreseeing and foretelling in the presence of the sick the present, the past and the future, and by explaining the omissions the patients have been guilty of, he will the more readily be believed to know their circumstances, so that men will have confidence to entrust themselves to him. The capacity is stated as covering all three tenses and not the future alone."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because the corpus states the capacity before stating any sign, and states it in terms of what it produces in the patient rather than in terms of what it establishes about the disease. The sentence that follows puts the order plainly: it is impossible to make all the sick well, and this would have been better than to be able to foretell what is going to happen. The capacity is named second-best by its own treatise."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1, in the English of Francis Antony Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 234-235; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, pp. 6-8" .

pmp:HippocratesPrologesis a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Announcing beforehand who will die and who will recover"@en, "Das vorherige Ansagen, wer stirbt und wer durchkommt"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "προλέγειν"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The operation the treatise prescribes: having formed a judgment from the whole of the signs, the physician says in advance, in the presence of the sick and of those around, which patients will die, which will recover, and in which cases the disease will be protracted. It is performed before the outcome and in public, and the treatise gives twenty-four chapters of signs as its means."@en ;
    skos:note "Separated from the capacity because the treatise separates them: chapter 1 says what the knowledge is for and chapter 25 says what the announcing requires, which is a judgment from the ensemble of the signs weighed against one another, together with the epidemic influx and the constitution of the season."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1 and 25, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 234-235 and 255-256" .

pmc:HippocratesAnaitios a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Being held not to be the cause"@en, "Nicht als die Ursache zu gelten"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "ἀναίτιος"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The state the treatise names as what the announcing secures for the physician: foreknowing and foretelling those who will die and those who will be saved, he would be blameless. The Greek word is the one used of a party who is not the cause of what happened; Adams renders the clause as escaping censure."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the capacity the opening chapter actually argues for, and it is not a capacity over the disease. The chapter has just conceded that some die before the physician is called and some immediately after, before he could bring his art to counteract the disease, and that it is therefore necessary to know how far such affections are above the powers of the constitution. The announcing is what converts that concession into a position: a death that was announced is a death the announcer is not answerable for."@en ;
    skos:note "What would tell against reading it this way. The same clause can be read as saying only that a physician who predicts correctly will not be blamed for predicting, which is a statement about reputation and not about liability. The Greek does not decide it: ἀναίτιος is used both ways. What does decide it is the sentence before, which is about cases the art cannot reach, and the words the translator chose, escape censure; both point at answerability for the outcome."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 235; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, p. 6" .

pmc:HippocratesPronoiaAnaitios a pm:Yielding ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The announcing is said to make the announcer blameless"@en, "Das Ansagen soll den Ansagenden unbelangbar machen"@de ;
    skos:definition "Stated in one clause of the opening chapter: by foreknowing and foretelling those who will die and those who will be saved, the physician would be blameless. The practice is the public announcement, the thing arrived at is the position of not being held the cause, and the treatise names no other return on the announcing in that chapter."@en ;
    skos:note "A yielding claim whose product falls on the operator and not on the patient, which is rare in the holding and is why it is entered as one. Nothing in the chapter says the announcing changes the course of the disease; the next sentence says the physician will be the better able to treat aright those who can be saved, and that is attributed to having anticipated everything, not to having said it aloud."@en ;
    skos:note "Its failure condition is weak and the treatise supplies no term for it. A wrong announcement is not said to cost the physician anything, and chapter 25 widens the signs to cover diseases it does not name, so there is no case in which the announcing is said to have been inadmissible. That is the shape SPEC §3 records as pm:claimDoesNotDiscriminate, and no protocol is entered here because none was carried."@en ;
    pm:byPractice pmp:HippocratesPrologesis ;
    pm:yieldsCapacity pmc:HippocratesAnaitios ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 1, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 235" .

pmc:HippocratesFaciesMoribunda a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The face by which death is said to be at hand"@en, "Das Gesicht, an dem der Tod nahe sein soll"@de ;
    skos:definition "The sign the chapter puts first and describes item by item: a sharp nose, hollow eyes, collapsed temples, the ears cold and contracted with their lobes turned out, the skin about the forehead rough and distended and parched, and the colour of the whole face green, black, livid or lead-coloured. The best countenance is stated in the same place and in the same form: like those of persons in health, and better still like itself."@en ;
    skos:note "A sign and not a cause, and the chapter is explicit that the inference runs from the ensemble: if the countenance be such at the commencement of the disease and this cannot be accounted for from the other symptoms, then the further questions follow. The eye signs of the same chapter are entered with it rather than separately, since the chapter states them as one examination: shunning the light, involuntary weeping, squinting, unequal size, red or livid whites or black veins in them, gum, restlessness, protrusion or hollowness; and, in sleep, a portion of the white appearing because the lids are not closed, which the chapter calls very deadly."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 2, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 235-236" .

pmc:HippocratesFaciesInterroganda a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The deadly face must first be asked after, and it is decided in a day and a night"@en, "Das tödliche Gesicht ist erst zu befragen, und es entscheidet sich in einem Tag und einer Nacht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The treatise defeats its own strongest sign in the sentence after stating it. Inquiry must be made whether the patient has long wanted sleep, whether his bowels have been very loose, and whether he has suffered from want of food; if any of these be confessed to, the danger is to be reckoned so far less, and it becomes obvious in the course of a day and a night whether or not the appearance proceeds from these causes. Only if none of these is said to exist, and the symptoms do not subside in that time, is death to be known for certain to be at hand."@en ;
    skos:note "The caution is what makes the sign a sign rather than a verdict, and it carries its own counted term: one day and one night, within which the alternative explanation either holds or falls. The holding has no other place where a sign is given a stated interval for being wrong."@en ;
    skos:note "Two further defeating conditions stand in the same treatise and are not entered separately. Chapter 2 excepts the sleeper who does not close his lids from habit, and the case where the open-lidded sleep goes with diarrhoea or a purgative; chapter 12 says the urine prognosis does not hold if the bladder itself is diseased. All three have the same form: the sign is withdrawn where another cause for it is available."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:HippocratesFaciesMoribunda ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 2, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 236" .

pmc:HippocratesDiesCritici a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The days on which fevers are said to come to a crisis"@en, "Die Tage, an denen Fieber zur Krise kommen sollen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "διὰ τεσσάρων ἐς τὰς εἴκοσιν ἐκ προσθέσιος"@grc ;
    skos:definition "A series stated as doctrine and not as a range of observations: fevers come to a crisis on the same days as to number on which men recover and die. The mildest end on the fourth day or earlier and the most malignant kill on the fourth day or earlier; the second class is protracted to the seventh, the third to the eleventh, the fourth to the fourteenth, the fifth to the seventeenth and the sixth to the twentieth, so that the periods ascend by fours up to twenty. After these, in the same progression, come periods of thirty-four, forty and sixty days. Quartans and the crises of women after parturition are said to follow the same order."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the one thing in the treatise that fixes a moment at which a prediction has either held or not, and because the holding already carries counted terms from four other transmissions and had none from this one. What rides on the number here is the course of a disease and not a site, which is the distinction the counter-comparandum at pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound turns on."@en ;
    skos:note "The series is in open dispute inside its own collection, and the dispute is not entered as a node because the competing places were not all opened. Section IV of the Aphorisms makes the critical days of sweats the third, fifth, seventh, ninth, eleventh, fourteenth, seventeenth, twenty-first, twenty-seventh and thirty-fourth, which has an odd-numbered base where this has tetrads and which carries a twenty-first and a twenty-seventh that this series does not. Adams states in his own discourse that the treatise On Critical Days is in all probability spurious, and reports Galen as declaring Hippocrates the first to have written on the subject while doubting that Galen had authority for it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 20, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 251-252; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, pp. 42-43" .

pmc:HippocratesNonIntegrisDiebus a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "None of these periods can be counted in whole days"@en, "Keine dieser Fristen lässt sich in ganzen Tagen zählen"@de ;
    skos:altLabel "οὐ δύναται δὲ ὅλῃσιν ἡμέρῃσιν ἀριθμεῖσθαι οὐδὲν τούτων ἀτρεκέως"@grc ;
    skos:definition "The sentence that follows the series, in the same chapter and in the same breath: none of these can be truly calculated by whole days, for neither the year nor the months can be numbered by entire days. The treatise states the numbers and then withdraws their precision, giving as its ground the incommensurability of the calendar itself."@en ;
    skos:note "A warning against the thing the passage prescribes, standing inside the prescribing passage, in the mouth of the writer who gave it, two sentences later. It is entered as its own node because it does what no other counted threshold in the holding does: it removes in advance the outcome that would have told against the series, namely a crisis that falls a day beside a stated day."@en ;
    skos:note "It is deliberately NOT added to the derivation of pmc:SelfWarningStandsInsideThePrescription, and the near-miss is worth recording rather than passing over. That statement is about a transmission warning the practitioner against valuing a practice for the wrong reason. This warns against the exactness of a count and not against a valuing, and the one who is relieved by it is the author. A successor statement about transmissions that state a number and withdraw its precision in the same passage would take this as a witness; the existing one does not, and stretching it to fit would make it unfalsifiable in exactly the way its own notes guard against."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmc:HippocratesDiesCritici ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 20, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 251; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, p. 42" .

pmc:HippocratesSignaOmniTempore a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "The signs are claimed for every year, every season and any disease not named"@en, "Die Zeichen werden für jedes Jahr, jede Jahreszeit und jede nicht genannte Krankheit beansprucht"@de ;
    skos:definition "The closing chapter extends the signs twice. In place: they are said to have held true in Libya, in Delos and in Scythia, from which it is said to be known that in the same regions many more things than these can be learned. In coverage: you should not complain because the name of any disease may happen not to be described here, for you may know all such as come to a crisis in the aforementioned times by the same symptoms."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered because it is the sentence that removes the last way the treatise could have been caught out. A sign catalogue that names its diseases can be tested against a disease it fails on; one that covers the diseases it does not name cannot. The three places are the other half: they are named as verification and no observation from any of them is reported."@en ;
    skos:note "The geographical claim is the only dating hook in the treatise and the reading of it is disputed. Littré reports Petersen taking Libya, Delos and Scythia as a travel record and building a biography on it, and holds the verification in those places at the time of writing to be very doubtful. Read through Littré's French and not opened at Petersen, so no node is entered for the dispute."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 25, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 256; the Greek from Hippocrates, vol. II, ed. and trans. W. H. S. Jones, Loeb Classical Library 148, London 1923, pp. 54-55" .

pmp:HippocratesUvulaeResectio a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Cutting or scarifying the enlarged uvula"@en, "Das Abtragen oder Skarifizieren der vergrößerten Uvula"@de ;
    skos:definition "The one operation the Prognostic prescribes with a time condition on it: the enlarged uvula is cut off or scarified, but only once it has changed in a stated way. The treatise distinguishes the variety it calls Uva, which is to be operated on when the whole of it is converted into an abscess, from the one it calls Columella, which is to be operated on when its extremity is larger and round and its upper part thinner."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" .

pmc:HippocratesUvulaRubraNonSecanda a pm:Cautioning ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Not while it is red and large"@en, "Nicht, solange sie rot und groß ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "Stated with its consequence in the same sentence: it is very dangerous to cut off or scarify enlarged uvulae while they are red and large, for inflammations and haemorrhages supervene; one should try to reduce such swellings by some other means at this season. When the change named in the same chapter has occurred, it will be safe to operate."@en ;
    skos:note "A prohibition whose condition is a state of the part and not a date, which is the form the holding has least of. The stated consequence is specific enough to be recognised by a bystander, which is what distinguishes it from a caution that can never be seen to have been justified."@en ;
    pm:cautionsAbout pmp:HippocratesUvulaeResectio ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" .

pmc:HippocratesUvulaAlvusAperienda a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Open the bowels before the uvula is cut, if there is time"@en, "Vor dem Schnitt an der Uvula den Leib öffnen, wenn Zeit bleibt"@de ;
    skos:definition "The order the chapter gives and the two conditions it attaches: it will be better to open the bowels gently before proceeding to the operation, if time will permit and the patient be not in danger of being suffocated. No consequence of omitting it is stated anywhere in the treatise."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at the weakest strength the scale carries a consequence for, and that is the finding about it. The chapter says better and not necessary, and it names two circumstances in which the step is to be dropped; a reading that made this a necessity would be lending ancient warrant to a sequence the source left conditional. SPEC §11 names that error directly."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HippocratesUvulaeResectio ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HippocratesAlvusAperienda ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" .

pmp:HippocratesAlvusAperienda a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:PrognosticonHippocratis ;
    rdfs:label "Opening the bowels gently"@en, "Das sanfte Öffnen des Leibes"@de ;
    skos:definition "Named in the same chapter only as a step preceding the operation on the uvula, and not described: the bowels are to be opened gently beforehand. The treatise gives neither the means nor a measure for it here, and it is entered as a node because the prerequisite claim above has to point at something that a later reader can go and look for in the rest of the collection."@en ;
    skos:note "What was read gives this no procedure of its own. The place where this corpus does describe evacuating is the Aphorisms, Section VI, already in this file at pmp:HippocratesIncisioEtCauterium and pmc:HippocratesEvacuatioCeleris, and that is about emptying an abscess and not the bowels. A run that opens On Regimen in Acute Diseases, which stands in this same volume, should look there."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, The Book of Prognostics 23, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 254" .

#
# — On Injuries of the Head, chapters 1 to 21, opened 2026-10-03 —
#
# This is the treatise the file named on 2026-10-02 as the strongest candidate
# in the corpus for a stated interval after an injury, and the one whose
# absence made the counter-comparandum a finding about the Aphorisms rather
# than about the collection. It has one, and the finding changes shape rather
# than collapsing. The four nodes below are the whole of it: the site orders
# the dying but is not given a number, the number is given and rides on the
# season, the invariance to the age of the wound is stated, and the failure
# the number measures is the surgeon's.
#

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisLocus a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "The site orders how long the dying takes, and is given no number"@en, "Der Ort ordnet die Dauer des Sterbens, und bekommt keine Zahl"@de ;
    skos:definition "The second chapter grades the skull by site and states the consequence of each grade comparatively. At the bregma the bone is thinnest and the flesh over it least and the brain beneath it greatest, so that from a similar or even smaller wound the bone is more contused, fractured and depressed, injuries there are more deadly and more difficult to cure, and a man will die in a shorter space of time than from a wound in any other part of the head. Behind the vertex and the ear the bone is stronger and the flesh deeper, so that in a fatal accident the patient will live longer than when the wound is elsewhere, and pus takes longer to form and penetrate to the brain. The temple is named as the other weak place, for the jaw joint, the organ of hearing and a hollow and important vein. Not one of these gradations carries a day-count."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the node the convergence at pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound needed and it is not the node the earlier reading expected. The Greek corpus does couple the wounded site to the duration of the dying; what it does not do is express the duration as a number. It says longer and shorter and sooner and later, over a graded list of sites, and it stops there."@en ;
    skos:note "The gradation is contested in the surgical literature and the contest is reported through the translator, so no dispute node is entered. Adams records that the posterior part being less dangerous is at variance with the experience of certain modern authorities and sets Guthrie's experience beside it as consonant; the Latin commentators Arantius and Porralius are quoted against chapter 18 on children in the strongest terms, that none was ever saved on whom the section was used. Both were read in Adams's notes and neither work was opened."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 2, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 446-447" .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisTempusAnni a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "The number rides on the season, and expressly not on the site"@en, "Die Zahl hängt an der Jahreszeit, und ausdrücklich nicht am Ort"@de ;
    skos:definition "Two statements, in the second and the nineteenth chapter, and they agree. Chapter 2: in fatal cases a man will survive longer in winter than in summer, whatever be the part of the head in which the wound is situated. Chapter 19, of a wound whose broken or contused bone was not discovered and which was neglected as if the bone were sound: fever will generally come on before the fourteenth day if in winter, and in summer the fever usually seizes after seven days; and they die before the end of seven days if in summer, and before fourteen if in winter."@en ;
    skos:note "The counted term exists in this corpus and it is indexed on the calendar. Chapter 2 does not merely leave the site unnumbered, it says in one clause that the thing which is counted does not depend on the site at all. The two chapters therefore divide the work between them: the site decides the order, the season decides the number."@en ;
    skos:note "What this does to the convergence candidate in prima-materia#612. The agreement between the needling-prohibition chapter of the Suwen and the marman chapter of the Susruta is not that a medical transmission couples a wound to a duration — the Greek does that too — but that the duration is given as a number indexed on the site. The Greek indexes the number on the season and the order on the site, and says so expressly. The note at pmc:TheIntervalAfterTheWound is corrected accordingly in the same bundle."@en ;
    skos:note "A second term stands in chapter 14 and is a window rather than an outcome: when the raspatory shows that the wound in the bone requires the operation, you must not postpone it for three days but do it during this period, more especially if the weather be hot. The season enters there as an intensifier and not as a boundary, which is why that place is entered as a prerequisite below and not as a term here."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 2 and 19, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 447 and 463-464" .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisSignaAequalia a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "The signs are declared the same whether the wound is old or recent"@en, "Die Zeichen gelten gleich, ob die Wunde alt oder frisch ist"@de ;
    skos:definition "After the sequence of signs that precede death in a neglected head wound — the wound losing its colour, the inflammation dying in it, the wound becoming glutinous and of a tawny and somewhat livid colour, the bone sphacelating and turning black and then pale, small blisters forming on the tongue, death in delirium, convulsions on the side opposite the wound, and in some an apoplexy — the chapter states: and these symptoms indicate, in the same manner, whether the wound be older or more recent."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered separately because it is an explicit invariance claim and the holding has few. It says that one variable which an observer would expect to matter does not, and it says it of the sign-set and not of the term. Together with the season clause it fixes what this treatise thinks the duration depends on: the time of year, and not the age of the wound and not the place of it."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 19, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 464" .

pmc:HippocratesVulnusCapitisCulpaMedici a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "The counted death follows from the surgeon's omission and not from the wound"@en, "Der gezählte Tod folgt aus dem Versäumnis des Arztes und nicht aus der Wunde"@de ;
    skos:definition "The condition the nineteenth chapter puts on its own term, stated before the numbers: when a bone is broken or cleft or contused or otherwise injured, and when by mistake it has not been discovered, and neither the raspatory nor the trepan has been applied as required, but the case has been neglected as if the bone were sound. The seven and fourteen days are the terms of that neglect. The chapter then names the escape: if you perceive that fever is coming on and any of these symptoms accompany it, you must not put off, but having sawed the bone to the membrane or scraped it, you must apply the other treatment."@en ;
    skos:note "The claim is entered because of where it puts the failure. The treatise opens its prognostic chapter by saying the physician may form an opinion of the patient's approaching dissolution and foretell what is to happen — which is the announcing of the Prognostic — and the case it describes is one the physician produced by missing the injury. So the same corpus that gives the announcing as what makes the announcer blameless gives, in the other treatise, a counted death whose stated cause is the announcer's own mistake. Both halves are in the holding now and neither is a reading of the other."@en ;
    skos:note "What would tell against this reading. The chapter can be read as describing an unrecognisable injury rather than a negligent surgeon: chapters 5 and 10 say at length that contusion and fine fissures cannot be recognised by sight and that the sounding probe does not show them. On that reading the mistake is excusable and the term is a property of the injury after all. The chapter's own words are by mistake it has not been discovered and neglected as if the bone were sound, which name an omission; the two readings are not reconciled here and both places are cited."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 19, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 463-464", "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 5 and 10, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 449 and 452-453" .

#
# The operation, and the five things the treatise forbids around it
#

pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "Trepanning the skull"@en, "Das Trepanieren des Schädels"@de ;
    skos:definition "The operation the treatise is written around: the bone is sawed or perforated to give issue to the blood and to reach the injury that the raspatory has shown. Two instruments are distinguished — a serrated or crown trepan which is used when the case is taken over from another and is carried at once down to the membrane, and a perforator which is not to be carried through — and the chapter prescribes a different procedure according to whether the surgeon has had the management of the case from the first."@en ;
    skos:note "The indication is stated as a limit and not as a licence, which is unusual enough to record: chapter 9 says that a hedra without fracture and contusion does not require trepanning, that a large and wide notch does not, and that those bones which are most pressed and broken require trepanning the least. Chapter 17 adds that depressed fragments are not to be interfered with."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 9, 14, 17, 18 and 21, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 451-452, 459, 462-463 and 465-466" .

pmc:HippocratesTrepanatioTriduum a pm:Presupposing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "Within the three days, and not after them"@en, "Innerhalb der drei Tage, nicht danach"@de ;
    skos:definition "The window the fourteenth chapter sets on the operation: when you scrape the bone with the raspatory and it appears that the wound in the bone requires the operation, you must not postpone it for three days but do it during this period, more especially if the weather be hot, and you have had the management of the treatment from the commencement. The scraping that establishes the indication is itself placed on the day after the wound is laid open and tented."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at prescribed order and not at stated necessity, and the reason is a decision about reading rather than caution. The chapter states no consequence for exceeding the three days. The nearest stated consequence in the same work is the term in chapter 19, and that one is attached to the injury never having been discovered at all, which is a different omission from an operation performed late. Reading chapter 19 as the consequence clause of chapter 14 would manufacture a stated necessity out of two places that do not refer to each other, and SPEC §3 keeps the scale apart for exactly that reason."@en ;
    skos:note "The season appears here as an intensifier — more especially if the weather be hot — and not as a boundary, and the second condition is about the surgeon and not the patient: the rule is given for the case where he has had the management from the commencement. Chapter 21 makes the same distinction govern the whole technique."@en ;
    pm:dependentStep pmp:HippocratesTrepanatio ;
    pm:priorStep pmp:HippocratesOssisRadulatio ;
    pm:prerequisiteStrength pm:prescribedOrder ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 14, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, p. 459" .

pmp:HippocratesOssisRadulatio a pm:Practicing ;
    pm:fromWork pmw:DeVulneribusCapitis ;
    rdfs:label "Scraping the bone with the raspatory"@en, "Das Abschaben des Knochens mit dem Schabeisen"@de ;
    skos:definition "The step that establishes whether the operation is required, and the treatise insists on it because the thing to be found cannot be seen. The bone is scraped to a depth and length proportionate to the wound and again transversely, for the sake of the fractures which are not seen and the contusions which are not discovered; where an indentation is present, the dint itself and the surrounding bone are scraped. The chapter also gives a staining step for the case that remains unclear: black ink is put into the wound, the wound is dressed, and on the next day the bone is cleaned and scraped again, when a fracture line takes the colour."@en ;
    skos:note "This is the one place in the two treatises where the corpus states a procedure for making an invisible finding visible, rather than a sign to be read off. It is also where the sounding probe is expressly ruled out: chapter 10 says the probe will not find out a contusion or a fissure that does not show, with the exception of sounding — that is, it names its own instrument as the wrong one for this question."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:HippocraticMedicine ;
    dcterms:source "Hippocrates, On Injuries of the Head 10 and 14, in the English of Francis Adams, The Genuine Works of Hippocrates, vol. I, London: printed for the Sydenham Society, 1849, pp. 452-453 and 459-460" .

